<section xml:id="sec-perpendicular-bisector-loci" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Perpendicular Bisector Loci</title>
    <objectives component="outcomes">
        <introduction>
            <p>
            <dl>
                <li>
                    <title>Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        2.0 Measurements and Geometry
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Sub-Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        2.3 Loci
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Specific Learning Outcomes</title> 
                    <p>
                        Relate terms used in loci to normal geometric constructions terms
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Grade Level</title>
                    <p>
                        12
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Estimated Duration</title>
                    <p>
                        40 minutes
                    </p>
                </li>
            </dl>
            </p>
        </introduction>
    </objectives>

    <!-- Planning notes (from the Combined Mathematics tab of the Grade 12 Book Structure Mapping sheet)
         Content summary: Loci terms; locus of points equidistant from two fixed points (perpendicular bisector).
         Suggested activity: On the school field, learners use a string to find all points equidistant from two fixed pegs and mark the perpendicular bisector with chalk.
         Interactive (JSXGraph): Two draggable points A and B; a point P traces all positions where PA = PB, building the perpendicular bisector. -->

    <!-- Section notes (2026-11)
         SLOs: Core 2.3 (a); (b) perpendicular-bisector half taught here (8.4 has the angle bisector)
         Activity: as planning notes (string and two pegs) | added: string lengths that do and do not meet
         JSXGraph: fig-perpendicular-bisector-locus (assets/jsxgraph/perpendicular-bisector-locus.js), as planning notes, with Next/Back construction steps as board buttons
         Key inquiry question: kiq-loci, placed in the section body (an introduction is not allowed in a section without subsections)
         STACK: assets/stack/top/2-measurement-and-geometry/08-loci/01-perpendicular-bisector-loci/ -->

    <question xml:id="kiq-loci">
        <p><em>How do we use loci to decide where things can be placed or where they can move?</em></p>
    </question>
    <p>
        A <em>locus</em> is the path a moving point can take, or the set of places where something is allowed to be. When a goat is tied to a peg, the places it can reach make a locus. When a county engineer wants a borehole to be fair to two villages, the places that are the same distance from both make a locus.
    </p>
    <p>
        In this chapter you describe loci in words, construct them with a ruler and a pair of compasses, and combine them to find the places that obey several rules at once: a borehole sited fairly between two villages, the area a security guard's floodlight covers, the angle a goalkeeper has to defend. The constructions are the ones you met in Grade 11; what is new is reading each one as a set of points that obey a rule.
    </p>

    <p component="lesson-plan">Upon completion of this lesson, learners will be able to:
        <ol>
            <li><p><alert>Know:</alert> a locus is every point that obeys a rule and no other point; the basic loci in locus language and in construction language; every point on the perpendicular bisector of <m>AB</m> is equidistant from <m>A</m> and <m>B</m>, and every such point is on it.</p></li>
            <li><p><alert>Do:</alert> construct the perpendicular bisector of a segment; decide whether a grid point is on it, nearer to <m>A</m> or nearer to <m>B</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p><alert>Apply:</alert> place an object fairly between two others, using a scale drawing.</p></li>
        </ol>
    </p>

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Problem-Solving and Discovery</title>
        <dl>
            <li><title>Suggested Time</title><p>14 minutes</p></li>
            <li><title>Objective</title><p>Learners find many points equidistant from two pegs and see that they form a straight line, and why the string must be longer than half the gap.</p></li>
            <li><title>Teacher's Role</title><p>Groups of four on the field with two pegs <m>6\,\text{m}</m> apart and two strings of equal length. If outside work is not possible, use paper with a scale of <m>1\,\text{cm}</m> to <m>1\,\text{m}</m> and a pair of compasses for the strings. Do not name <q>perpendicular bisector</q> until part (e). After part (d), ask: <q>Why did the <m>2.5\,\text{m}</m> strings never meet?</q></p></li>
        </dl>
    </axiom>

    <activity xml:id="act-two-pegs-on-the-field">
        <title>Two Pegs on the Field</title>
        <statement>
            <p>Two pegs <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> are pushed into the school field <m>6\,\text{m}</m> apart. Two learners each hold a string of the same length, one from <m>A</m> and one from <m>B</m>, and pull them tight. A third learner marks with chalk where the ends of the strings meet.</p>
            <p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>Use strings <m>5\,\text{m}</m> long. Where can the strings meet? How far is each meeting point from the middle <m>M</m> of <m>AB</m>?</p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>At two points, one on each side of <m>AB</m>. Each is <m>\sqrt{5^2 - 3^2} = 4\,\text{m}</m> from <m>M</m> (a <m>3, 4, 5</m> triangle).</p></solution>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>Repeat with strings <m>4\,\text{m}</m> long.</p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>Two points, each <m>\sqrt{4^2 - 3^2} = \sqrt{7} \approx 2.6\,\text{m}</m> from <m>M</m>.</p></solution>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>Repeat with strings <m>3\,\text{m}</m> long.</p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>Only one point: the strings meet at <m>M</m> itself.</p></solution>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>Repeat with strings <m>2.5\,\text{m}</m> long.</p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>They do not meet: <m>2.5\,\text{m}</m> is less than half of <m>6\,\text{m}</m>, so the two strings together cannot reach across <m>AB</m>.</p></solution>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>Mark all your points with chalk. What shape do they make, and how does it cross <m>AB</m>?</p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>A straight line through <m>M</m>, at right angles to <m>AB</m>.</p></solution>
                        <note component="lesson-plan"><p>This is the moment to name the line, in two languages: in locus language it is <q>the points equidistant from <m>A</m> and <m>B</m></q>; in construction language it is <q>the perpendicular bisector of <m>AB</m></q>.</p></note>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </activity>

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Structured Instruction</title>
        <p>
            <dl>
                <li><title>Suggested Time</title><p>8 minutes</p></li>
                <li><title>Objective</title><p>To formalise the idea of a locus, the two languages for loci, and the perpendicular bisector locus.</p></li>
            </dl>
        </p>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Key Takeaways &amp; Teacher Connection</title>
            <ol>
                <li><p><alert>Connecting to Learner Work:</alert> every chalk mark was the same distance from both pegs, and every point the same distance from both pegs could have been marked: that is what makes the line a locus.</p></li>
                <li><p>The table of locus language and construction language is the map of the whole chapter.</p></li>
                <li><p>The string length is the compass radius. It must be more than half of <m>AB</m>, or the arcs do not meet.</p></li>
                <li><p>The construction works because each crossing point is one radius from <m>A</m> and the same radius from <m>B</m>. Recall the Grade 11 circumcircle construction, which used two perpendicular bisectors.</p></li>
            </ol>
        </paragraphs>
    </axiom>

    <insight xml:id="insight-loci-in-two-languages">
        <title>Loci in Two Languages</title>
        <p>
            A <term>locus</term> (plural <term>loci</term>) is the set of all points, and only those points, that obey a rule. We often say <q>the locus of a point <m>P</m> that moves so that ...</q>. Every point on the locus obeys the rule, and every point that obeys the rule is on the locus. A locus may be a line, a curve or a region. Points that are the same distance from two things are <term>equidistant</term> from them.
        </p>
        <table xml:id="table-locus-and-construction-language">
            <title>Locus Language and Construction Language</title>
            <tabular halign="left" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
                <row header="yes" bottom="medium"><cell>Locus language: the points that are ...</cell><cell>Construction language</cell><cell>Section</cell></row>
                <row><cell>equidistant from two points <m>A</m> and <m>B</m></cell><cell>the perpendicular bisector of <m>AB</m></cell><cell><xref ref="sec-perpendicular-bisector-loci"/></cell></row>
                <row><cell>a fixed distance <m>r</m> from a point <m>O</m></cell><cell>a circle, centre <m>O</m>, radius <m>r</m></cell><cell><xref ref="sec-loci-at-a-given-distance-from-a-fixed-point"/></cell></row>
                <row><cell>a fixed distance <m>d</m> from a straight line <m>l</m></cell><cell>a pair of lines parallel to <m>l</m>, one on each side, each <m>d</m> from <m>l</m></cell><cell><xref ref="sec-loci-at-a-given-distance-from-a-fixed-line"/></cell></row>
                <row><cell>equidistant from two parallel lines</cell><cell>the line parallel to both, halfway between them</cell><cell><xref ref="sec-loci-at-a-given-distance-from-a-fixed-line"/></cell></row>
                <row><cell>equidistant from two intersecting lines</cell><cell>the two bisectors of the angles between the lines</cell><cell><xref ref="sec-angle-bisector-loci"/></cell></row>
                <row><cell>at which a segment <m>AB</m> is seen at a fixed angle <m>\theta</m></cell><cell>two arcs of circles through <m>A</m> and <m>B</m>, mirror images in <m>AB</m></cell><cell><xref ref="sec-constant-angle-loci"/></cell></row>
            </tabular>
        </table>
        <p>
            In this chapter <q>construct</q> means using only a ruler (as a straight edge) and a pair of compasses; <q>draw</q> allows measuring with a ruler and a protractor. Never rub out construction arcs. Measure lengths on drawings to the nearest millimetre and angles to the nearest degree.
        </p>
    </insight>

    <insight xml:id="insight-perpendicular-bisector-locus">
        <title>The Perpendicular Bisector Locus</title>
        <p>
            The locus of points equidistant from two points <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> is the <term>perpendicular bisector</term> of <m>AB</m>: the line through the midpoint of <m>AB</m> at right angles to <m>AB</m>. Both halves hold: every point on the perpendicular bisector is equidistant from <m>A</m> and <m>B</m>, and every point equidistant from <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> is on it.
        </p>
        <p>To construct it:</p>
        <p>
            <ol>
                <li><p>Open the compasses to more than half of <m>AB</m>.</p></li>
                <li><p>With the point at <m>A</m>, draw an arc above <m>AB</m> and an arc below it.</p></li>
                <li><p>Without changing the compasses, do the same from <m>B</m>.</p></li>
                <li><p>Join the two points where the arcs cross. Keep the arcs.</p></li>
            </ol>
        </p>
    </insight>

    <figure xml:id="fig-perpendicular-bisector-construction">
        <caption>Constructing the perpendicular bisector of <m>AB</m>.</caption>
        <image width="60%">
            <shortdescription>Segment AB of 7 cm with arcs of radius 5 cm drawn from A and from B, crossing above and below AB. The line through the two crossing points meets AB at its midpoint M at right angles, with AM and MB marked equal. A point P on the line is joined to A and B by dashed lines marked equal.</shortdescription>
            <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.8]
% SET
\tkzDefPoint(0,0){A} \tkzDefPoint(7,0){B}
% CALCULATE
\tkzDefMidPoint(A,B) \tkzGetPoint{M}
\tkzDefPoint(3.5,3.5707){C} \tkzDefPoint(3.5,-3.5707){D} % crossings: sqrt(5^2 - 3.5^2) = 3.57 from M
\tkzDefPoint(3.5,2){P}
% DRAW
\tkzDrawSegment[thick](A,B)
\draw (A) ++(28:5) arc (28:62:5);
\draw (A) ++(-62:5) arc (-62:-28:5);
\draw (B) ++(118:5) arc (118:152:5);
\draw (B) ++(-152:5) arc (-152:-118:5);
\tkzDrawLine[add=0.15 and 0.15](C,D)
\tkzDrawSegments[dashed](P,A P,B)
\tkzDrawPoints(A,B,M,P,C,D)
% MARK
\tkzMarkRightAngle[size=0.3](B,M,C)
\tkzMarkSegments[mark=|](A,M M,B)
\tkzMarkSegments[mark=||](P,A P,B)
% LABEL
\tkzLabelPoints[below left](A) \tkzLabelPoints[below right](B,M)
\tkzLabelPoints[right](P)
\end{tikzpicture}
            </latex-image>
        </image>
    </figure>

    <figure xml:id="fig-perpendicular-bisector-locus">
        <caption>Drag <m>P</m> until <m>PA = PB</m> and it leaves a dot. Find many such points, then tick <q>Show the locus</q>. Use <alert>Next</alert> and <alert>Back</alert> to see the construction.</caption>
        <interactive xml:id="interactive-perpendicular-bisector-locus" platform="jsxgraph" width="90%" aspect="16:12" source="jsxgraph/perpendicular-bisector-locus.js">
            <slate xml:id="jsxgraph-perpendicular-bisector-locus" surface="jsxboard" aspect="16:12"/>
            <instructions>
                <p>
                    <ul>
                        <li><p>Drag <m>P</m> and watch <m>PA</m> and <m>PB</m>. When they are equal, <m>P</m> leaves a dot. Find at least six dots, on both sides of <m>AB</m>.</p></li>
                        <li><p>Tick <alert>Show the locus</alert>. Do your dots lie on the line?</p></li>
                        <li><p>Press <alert>Next</alert> three times to see the compass construction, step by step. Drag <m>A</m> or <m>B</m> and see the construction follow.</p></li>
                        <li><p>Press <alert>Clear</alert> to remove the dots.</p></li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </instructions>
        </interactive>
    </figure>

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/example-intro.ptx" />

    <example xml:id="example-constructing-perpendicular-bisector">
        <title>Constructing the Perpendicular Bisector</title>
        <statement>
            <p>Draw a line segment <m>AB = 7\,\text{cm}</m> and construct its perpendicular bisector, with the compasses opened to <m>5\,\text{cm}</m>. How far from the midpoint <m>M</m> are the points where the arcs cross? Would a radius of <m>3\,\text{cm}</m> work?</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                <alert>Step 1:</alert> Draw <m>AB = 7\,\text{cm}</m>. Open the compasses to <m>5\,\text{cm}</m>, which is more than half of <m>AB</m> (<m>3.5\,\text{cm}</m>).
            </p>
            <p>
                <alert>Step 2:</alert> With the point at <m>A</m>, draw an arc above <m>AB</m> and an arc below. Without changing the compasses, do the same from <m>B</m>. Join the two crossing points (<xref ref="fig-perpendicular-bisector-construction"/>).
            </p>
            <p>
                <alert>Check:</alert> each crossing point is <m>5\,\text{cm}</m> from <m>A</m> and <m>3.5\,\text{cm}</m> along from <m>A</m> to <m>M</m>, so by Pythagoras its distance from <m>M</m> is
                <md>\sqrt{5^2 - 3.5^2} = \sqrt{12.75} \approx 3.6\,\text{cm}.</md>
                Measure it on your drawing. Any radius more than <m>3.5\,\text{cm}</m> works. A radius of <m>3\,\text{cm}</m> does not: the arcs from <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> would not meet.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="example-on-the-perpendicular-bisector">
        <title>On the Locus, or Nearer to <m>A</m> or <m>B</m>?</title>
        <statement>
            <p><m>A</m> is <m>(1, 2)</m> and <m>B</m> is <m>(7, 6)</m>. Find the midpoint <m>M</m> of <m>AB</m>. For each of <m>P(2, 7)</m>, <m>Q(6, 1)</m> and <m>R(5, 5)</m>, decide whether it is on the locus of points equidistant from <m>A</m> and <m>B</m>, nearer to <m>A</m>, or nearer to <m>B</m>.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                <m>M = \left(\frac{1 + 7}{2}, \frac{2 + 6}{2}\right) = (4, 4)</m>. Compare the squares of the distances, worked out by Pythagoras:
                <md>
                    <mrow>PA^2 \amp = 1^2 + 5^2 = 26, \amp PB^2 \amp = 5^2 + 1^2 = 26</mrow>
                    <mrow>QA^2 \amp = 5^2 + 1^2 = 26, \amp QB^2 \amp = 1^2 + 5^2 = 26</mrow>
                    <mrow>RA^2 \amp = 4^2 + 3^2 = 25, \amp RB^2 \amp = 2^2 + 1^2 = 5</mrow>
                </md>
                So <m>P</m> and <m>Q</m> are on the locus, and <m>R</m> is nearer to <m>B</m>. Comparing squares means nothing has to be rounded. <xref ref="fig-grid-perpendicular-bisector"/> shows the points.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-grid-perpendicular-bisector">
                <caption>The perpendicular bisector of <m>AB</m>, with <m>A(1, 2)</m> and <m>B(7, 6)</m>.</caption>
                <image width="55%">
                    <shortdescription>A grid from 0 to 8 in each direction with A(1, 2), B(7, 6) and their midpoint M(4, 4). The perpendicular bisector of AB is a dashed line through M. P(2, 7) and Q(6, 1) lie on it; R(5, 5) is on B's side of it.</shortdescription>
                    <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.6]
% SET
\coordinate (A) at (1,2); \coordinate (B) at (7,6); \coordinate (M) at (4,4);
\coordinate (P) at (2,7); \coordinate (Q) at (6,1); \coordinate (R) at (5,5);
% DRAW
\draw[gray!40] (0,0) grid (8,8);
\draw[->] (0,0) -- (8.5,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,0) -- (0,8.5) node[above] {$y$};
\foreach \k in {2,4,6,8} { \node[below] at (\k,0) {\small $\k$}; \node[left] at (0,\k) {\small $\k$}; }
\draw[thick] (A) -- (B);
\draw[thick,dashed] (4/3,8) -- (20/3,0);
% MARK
\foreach \p in {A,B,M,P,Q,R} \fill (\p) circle (2.5pt);
% LABEL
\node[below right] at (A) {$A$}; \node[above left] at (B) {$B$}; \node[below left] at (M) {$M$};
\node[left] at (P) {$P$}; \node[right] at (Q) {$Q$}; \node[above right] at (R) {$R$};
\end{tikzpicture}
                    </latex-image>
                </image>
            </figure>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="example-water-tank-between-homes">
        <title>A Water Tank Between Two Homes</title>
        <statement>
            <p>Two homes <m>H</m> and <m>K</m> are <m>80\,\text{m}</m> apart beside a straight path that joins them. A water tank is to be the same distance from both homes and <m>30\,\text{m}</m> from the path. Make a scale drawing with <m>1\,\text{cm}</m> representing <m>10\,\text{m}</m>, and find how far the tank is from each home.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                <alert>Step 1:</alert> Draw <m>HK = 8\,\text{cm}</m> and construct its perpendicular bisector. The tank is on this line, because it is equidistant from <m>H</m> and <m>K</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
                <alert>Step 2:</alert> Mark the tank <m>T</m> on the bisector <m>3\,\text{cm}</m> from <m>HK</m>. Measure <m>TH</m>: about <m>5.0\,\text{cm}</m>, which represents <m>50\,\text{m}</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
                <alert>Check:</alert> the midpoint of <m>HK</m> is <m>40\,\text{m}</m> from each home, so <m>TH = \sqrt{40^2 + 30^2} = 50\,\text{m}</m>. The tank is <m>50\,\text{m}</m> from each home. (There is a second possible place, <m>30\,\text{m}</m> on the other side of the path.)
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <note xml:id="note-loci-with-geometry-software">
        <title>Loci with Geometry Software</title>
        <p>
            Free geometry software such as GeoGebra lets you place two points <m>A</m> and <m>B</m>, construct the perpendicular bisector with one tool, then put a point on it and drag it while the software shows <m>PA</m> and <m>PB</m>. Try it on a phone or computer: the two lengths stay equal however far you drag. Checking a construction this way is a useful digital skill.
        </p>
    </note>

