    <exercises xml:id="exercises-perpendicular-bisector-loci">
        <exercise xml:id="ex-construct-perpendicular-bisector">
            <statement><p>Draw a line segment <m>AB = 9\,\text{cm}</m> and construct its perpendicular bisector with the compasses opened to <m>6\,\text{cm}</m>. How far from the midpoint <m>M</m> are the points where your arcs cross?</p></statement>
            <answer><p>About <m>4.0\,\text{cm}</m></p></answer>
            <solution><p>Each crossing point is <m>6\,\text{cm}</m> from <m>A</m>, and <m>AM = 4.5\,\text{cm}</m>, so its distance from <m>M</m> is <m>\sqrt{6^2 - 4.5^2} = \sqrt{15.75} \approx 3.97\,\text{cm}</m>, about <m>4.0\,\text{cm}</m>.</p></solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="ex-grid-perpendicular-bisector">
            <statement><p><m>A</m> is <m>(-2, 1)</m> and <m>B</m> is <m>(4, 3)</m>. Find the midpoint <m>M</m> of <m>AB</m>. For each of <m>P(0, 5)</m>, <m>Q(2, -1)</m> and <m>R(3, 1)</m>, decide whether it is on the locus of points equidistant from <m>A</m> and <m>B</m>, nearer to <m>A</m>, or nearer to <m>B</m>.</p></statement>
            <answer><p><m>M(1, 2)</m>; <m>P</m> and <m>Q</m> are on the locus; <m>R</m> is nearer to <m>B</m>.</p></answer>
            <solution><p><m>PA^2 = 4 + 16 = 20</m>, <m>PB^2 = 16 + 4 = 20</m>: on. <m>QA^2 = 16 + 4 = 20</m>, <m>QB^2 = 4 + 16 = 20</m>: on. <m>RA^2 = 25 + 0 = 25</m>, <m>RB^2 = 1 + 4 = 5</m>: nearer to <m>B</m>.</p></solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="ex-kiosk-between-stages">
            <statement><p>Two matatu stages <m>S</m> and <m>T</m> are <m>1.2\,\text{km}</m> apart on a straight road. A kiosk must be equidistant from <m>S</m> and <m>T</m> and <m>0.8\,\text{km}</m> from the road. Make a scale drawing with <m>1\,\text{cm}</m> representing <m>0.2\,\text{km}</m>. How far is the kiosk from each stage?</p></statement>
            <answer><p><m>1.0\,\text{km}</m></p></answer>
            <solution><p>Draw <m>ST = 6\,\text{cm}</m> and construct its perpendicular bisector. Mark the kiosk on it <m>4\,\text{cm}</m> from <m>ST</m>. It measures <m>5\,\text{cm}</m> from each stage, which is <m>1.0\,\text{km}</m>. Check: <m>\sqrt{0.6^2 + 0.8^2} = 1.0\,\text{km}</m>.</p></solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="ex-length-from-the-bisector">
            <statement><p><m>P</m> is on the perpendicular bisector of <m>AB</m>, <m>5\,\text{cm}</m> from <m>AB</m>, and <m>PA = 13\,\text{cm}</m>. Find the length of <m>AB</m>.</p></statement>
            <answer><p><m>24\,\text{cm}</m></p></answer>
            <solution><p>If <m>M</m> is the midpoint of <m>AB</m>, triangle <m>PMA</m> has a right angle at <m>M</m>, so <m>AM = \sqrt{13^2 - 5^2} = 12\,\text{cm}</m> and <m>AB = 24\,\text{cm}</m>.</p></solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="ex-two-languages">
            <statement>
                <p>Write each locus in construction language.</p>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li><p>The locus of a point that is always as far from the gate <m>G</m> as from the tap <m>T</m>.</p></li>
                        <li><p>The locus of a point <m>2\,\text{m}</m> from the tap <m>T</m>.</p></li>
                        <li><p>The locus of a point equidistant from two parallel fences.</p></li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer><p>(a) The perpendicular bisector of <m>GT</m>. (b) A circle, centre <m>T</m>, radius <m>2\,\text{m}</m>. (c) The line parallel to both fences, halfway between them.</p></answer>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="ex-why-the-bisector-works">
            <title>Challenge: Why the Bisector Works</title>
            <statement><p><m>P</m> is equidistant from <m>A</m> and <m>B</m>, and <m>M</m> is the midpoint of <m>AB</m>. Show that <m>PM</m> is at right angles to <m>AB</m>.</p></statement>
            <hint><p>Compare triangles <m>PMA</m> and <m>PMB</m>.</p></hint>
            <answer><p>The triangles are congruent (SSS), so the angles at <m>M</m> are equal, and each is <m>90^{\circ}</m>.</p></answer>
            <solution><p>In triangles <m>PMA</m> and <m>PMB</m>: <m>PA = PB</m> (given), <m>AM = MB</m> (<m>M</m> is the midpoint) and <m>PM</m> is common. So the triangles are congruent (SSS), and angle <m>PMA</m> equals angle <m>PMB</m>. These two angles lie on the straight line <m>AB</m>, so they add to <m>180^{\circ}</m>, and each is <m>90^{\circ}</m>.</p></solution>
        </exercise>
    </exercises>

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Differentiation</title>
        <table xml:id="table-differentiation-perpendicular-bisector-loci">
            <title>Differentiation Strategies</title>
            <tabular halign="left" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
                <row header="yes" bottom="medium"><cell>Learner Group</cell><cell>Strategy and Learning Experience</cell></row>
                <row><cell>Struggling Learners (Support)</cell><cell>Do activity parts (a) to (c) on paper with a printed segment. Give a card with the three construction steps (radius more than half, arcs from both ends, join the crossings). Work <xref ref="example-constructing-perpendicular-bisector"/> together. Assign <xref ref="ex-construct-perpendicular-bisector"/> and <xref ref="ex-two-languages"/>.</cell></row>
                <row><cell>On-Level Learners (Core)</cell><cell>Full activity and all examples. Assign <xref ref="ex-construct-perpendicular-bisector"/> to <xref ref="ex-two-languages"/>.</cell></row>
                <row><cell>Advanced Learners (Challenge)</cell><cell>All exercises including <xref ref="ex-why-the-bisector-works"/>. Extension: find all points with whole-number coordinates between <m>x = 0</m> and <m>x = 8</m> that are equidistant from <m>(0, 0)</m> and <m>(8, 4)</m>. (Answer: the bisector is <m>y = -2x + 10</m>, so <m>(0, 10)</m>, <m>(1, 8)</m>, <m>(2, 6)</m>, <m>(3, 4)</m>, <m>(4, 2)</m>, <m>(5, 0)</m>, <m>(6, -2)</m>, <m>(7, -4)</m>, <m>(8, -6)</m>.)</cell></row>
            </tabular>
        </table>
    </axiom>

